The driver supports USB 2.0 wireless adapters based on the Ralink
RT2700U,  RT2800U and RT3000U chipsets.  The RT2700U chipset con‐
sists of two integrated chips, an RT2770 MAC/BBP  and  an  RT2720
(1T2R) or RT2750 (dual‐band 1T2R) radio transceiver.  The RT2800U
chipset  consists  of two integrated chips, an RT2870 MAC/BBP and
an RT2820 (2T3R) or RT2850 (dual‐band  2T3R)  radio  transceiver.
The  RT3000U is a single‐chip solution based on an RT3070 MAC/BBP
and an RT3020 (1T1R), RT3021  (1T2R),  RT3022  (2T2R)  or  RT3052
(dual‐band 2T2R) radio transceiver.  These are the modes the dri‐
ver can operate in: Also known as mode, this is used when associ‐
ating  with  an  access  point, through which all traffic passes.
This mode is the default.  In this mode the driver is able to re‐
ceive packets without associating with  an  access  point.   This
disables the internal receive filter and enables the card to cap‐
ture packets from networks which it wouldn’t normally have access
to,  or  to scan for access points.  The driver can be configured
to use Wired Equivalent Privacy (WEP) or Wi‐Fi  Protected  Access
(WPA‐PSK  and WPA2‐PSK).  WPA is the de facto encryption standard
for wireless networks.  It is strongly recommended that  WEP  not
be  used  as the sole mechanism to secure wireless communication,
due to serious weaknesses in it.  The driver  offloads  both  en‐
cryption  and  decryption  of data frames to the hardware for the
WEP40, WEP104, TKIP(+MIC) and CCMP ciphers.  The  driver  can  be
